-- Q1822: Attestation Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Based on our review, nothing came to our attention requiring modification","In our opinion, the subject matter is presented in all material respects","We make no representation regarding the subject matter presented","The procedures performed and findings obtained are as follows"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 205.57, an unqualified examination opinion uses positive assurance language: "In our opinion, [the subject matter] is presented, in all material respects, based on [the criteria]." This is the highest level of assurance in an attestation engagement.
Wrong (A): Negative assurance language ("nothing came to our attention") is used in review-level engagements under AT-C 210, not examinations.
Wrong (C): A statement making no representation describes a disclaimer, not an unqualified examination opinion.
Wrong (D): Reporting procedures and findings describes an agreed-upon procedures engagement under AT-C 215, not an examination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1822;

-- Q1823: Attestation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Positive assurance expressing an opinion on the subject matter","Negative assurance that nothing indicates material modifications needed","No assurance where the practitioner merely presents factual findings","A guarantee of the accuracy and completeness of the subject matter"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 210, a review attestation engagement provides limited (negative) assurance. The conclusion states that based on the review, nothing came to the practitioner''s attention that causes them to believe the subject matter is not presented in accordance with the criteria.
Wrong (A): Positive assurance ("In our opinion...") is the form used in examination engagements under AT-C 205, not review engagements.
Wrong (C): Presenting findings without assurance describes an agreed-upon procedures engagement under AT-C 215, not a review.
Wrong (D): No attestation engagement provides a guarantee. Even examinations provide reasonable, not absolute, assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1823;

-- Q1824: Attestation Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reasonable assurance in the form of an opinion on the matter","Limited assurance based on review-level procedures performed","No assurance; only a report of findings from procedures performed","A compilation of the subject matter with no procedures applied"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C 215.22, in an agreed-upon procedures engagement the practitioner performs only those specific procedures agreed upon by the engaging party and reports the findings. No assurance is provided; users draw their own conclusions from the factual findings per AT-C 215.34.
Wrong (A): Reasonable assurance with an opinion is characteristic of an examination engagement under AT-C 205, not an AUP engagement.
Wrong (B): Limited assurance is provided in a review engagement under AT-C 210. AUP engagements do not provide any level of assurance.
Wrong (D): A compilation involves presenting information in financial statement form. AUP engagements involve performing and reporting on specific procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1824;

-- Q1831: Attestation Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The historical statements have been audited or reviewed with proper adjustments","The pro forma adjustments are mathematically correct and assumptions reasonable","The entity will definitely complete the proposed business transaction","All pro forma statements are prepared in accordance with IFRS standards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 310.10, when examining pro forma financial information, the practitioner should obtain satisfaction that the underlying historical financial statements have been audited or reviewed and that the pro forma adjustments give appropriate effect to the significant transaction.
Wrong (B): While mathematical accuracy matters, AT-C 310 requires more than just verifying calculations. The practitioner must also confirm the historical statements have been subjected to audit or review.
Wrong (C): Pro forma information is inherently hypothetical. The practitioner does not evaluate whether the proposed transaction will be completed.
Wrong (D): Pro forma financial information follows the entity''s applicable framework, not necessarily IFRS. AT-C 310 does not mandate a specific framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1831;

-- Q1833: Attestation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unqualified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph","Modify the opinion to qualified or adverse based on pervasiveness","Disclaim an opinion due to the material instance of noncompliance","Issue the report as an agreed-upon procedures engagement instead"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 315, when a practitioner identifies material noncompliance in a compliance examination, the opinion must be modified. A qualified opinion is issued if the noncompliance is material but not pervasive; an adverse opinion is issued if it is both material and pervasive.
Wrong (A): An unqualified opinion with an emphasis paragraph would not adequately address material noncompliance. Opinion modification is required under AT-C 315.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is appropriate when scope limitations prevent the practitioner from obtaining sufficient evidence, not when noncompliance has been identified.
Wrong (D): Changing the engagement type to agreed-upon procedures is not an appropriate response to discovered noncompliance in an examination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1833;

-- Q1839: Attestation Engagements [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Suitably supported and provide a reasonable basis for the statements","Guaranteed to produce accurate and verifiable financial results","Identical to the assumptions used by competitors in the industry","Approved by the entity''s external auditor before the examination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 305.20, in an examination of prospective financial statements, the practitioner evaluates whether the assumptions provide a reasonable basis for the forecast and are suitably supported by available information. The assumptions must be internally consistent.
Wrong (B): No examination guarantees accuracy of future results. Prospective statements are inherently uncertain, and the practitioner assesses reasonableness, not certainty.
Wrong (C): Competitor assumptions are not a relevant benchmark. The practitioner evaluates the entity''s own assumptions based on its specific circumstances.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement for external auditor approval of assumptions. The practitioner independently evaluates the assumptions during the examination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1839;

